Derive a uniaxial response from force geometry and an empirical law (@engineering-axial-law: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/materials/Load-extension-stiffness-and-recoverable-deformation.lingua#r_48SM2DMGKVVHE2EVHKFN03NW8J") { r_00D14518ED004AB4B636D0A263
Take a uniform straight member of reference length L>0 and area A>0. Cut it across the axis and define positive section force N as tension. With no intermediate axial load, quasi-static force balance gives the same N along its length. For the uniform uniaxial approximation, nominal axial stress is σ=N/A. If its endpoints move apart by δ, the mean engineering strain is ε=δ/L. These definitions do not yet predict a deformation.

The additional empirical model is σ=E(ε−ε₀), with E>0 and a prescribed uniform free strain ε₀. The model is linear elastic in mechanical strain ε−ε₀ over its stated range. It is not deduced from force balance and is not made valid by choosing a small numerical step. Set g=Lε₀, the free extension relative to the chosen reference. Substitution gives N=(EA/L)(δ−g)=k(δ−g), with k=EA/L>0. Thus δ=g+N/k. Stress is in Pa, strain is dimensionless, g and δ are lengths, and k has units N/m.

For ε₀=0, doubling length halves k; doubling area or modulus doubles k. A larger stiffness can therefore come from a different material or a larger section, with different mass, processing and interface consequences. The same k does not identify E,A,L separately. A changing force–extension slope can result from changing geometry, an evolving material state, slipping contacts or another omitted interaction; fitting a new E is not automatically the right interpretation.

The free strain may represent an imposed small thermal strain αΔT when the coefficient, temperature and reference are specified, or an explicitly modeled assembly mismatch. At N=0 the member extends by g. At δ=0 it carries N=−kg. Zero displacement therefore does not imply zero force, and zero force does not imply zero strain. Changing g during a loading path introduces another energy exchange; the later fixed-g work argument cannot be reused unchanged.

**Derive and challenge:** recover the existing worked bar calculation in [Load, extension, stiffness and recoverable deformation](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/materials/Load-extension-stiffness-and-recoverable-deformation.lingua#r_48SM2DMGKVVHE2EVHKFN03NW8J) from these definitions. State the observation needed to distinguish E from support stiffness. Explain why a yield/proof stress cannot be obtained from E alone. Read [Stress–strain measures and the tensile test](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/materials/Stress-strain-measures-and-the-tensile-test.lingua#r_61ZDKD8MXWTDZ7Q1M9SECY1Z5P) for the distinction between reference and current geometry; large-deformation conversions and local multiaxial behavior remain later preparation. The common investigation will use specified coefficients rather than claim a real alloy's properties.

